As a programmer (yes, I have a degree in CS, and yes, I get paid to
program), I regularly use the term "bug" to refer to any *incorrect*
behavior of a program.  While working on QA, we would determine
certain behavior to be bugs despite the fact that the programmer had
originally intentionally coded them to do exactly what they were
doing, once we determinned (esp. by talking with the customer) that
the behavior was wrong.  Sometimes what was originally considered to
be a "feature" turns out, upon later review, to be incorrect--and thus
gets labelled a "bug".

Looking at the code, there are certain circumstances under which your
pet will not attack something:
1) The monster is more than one level higher than the pet.
2) If the monster is a non-invisible floating eyewhich can see the
pet, the pet has eyes & can see, 90% of the time the pet will not
attack.
3) If the monster is a gelatinous cube, there is another 90% chance
for no attack.
4) The pet would potentially suffer more damage from the monster's
passive attacks than its current hitpoints.
5) The monster is peaceful, the player is not wearing a ring of
conflict, and the pet has less than 25% of its maximum hitpoints OR
the target is the quest leader or one of his quards.
6) The pet does not resist stoning, and the monster has a stoning
touch.

Note that pets are less likely to attack floating eyes than peaceful
creatures.  The only peacefuls that are safe from a pet are the quest
leader and his guards; obviously, if this were not so, pacifists and
others with strong pets could have massive difficulties just getting
onto the second level of the quest.  So the dev team had to place an
exception.  If, on the other hand, pets did not generically attack
peacefuls, this exception would not be necessary.

Are we to believe that all pets (including, say, little dogs and
housecats) are bloodthirsty, barely tamed beasts?  As I've said many
times throughout this thread, this makes no sense to me.  Thus,
condition 5, although very explicitly coded by the dev team, makes no
sense, and I've changed it in my version.

If you find my arguments unconvincing, very well--I won't require you
to use my patch.  If you find them confusing, please let me know how
and where; I'll strive to explain myself better.

